hen `or {}` makes it `{}`.\n\nTo make `_parse_json` fail, `trans_options_str` must be invalid JSON. But `js_to_json` is quite lenient... Let me think. `js_to_json` handles comments, single quotes, unquoted keys, trailing commas? Let me check.\n\nActually, in the real UDN page, the options might be like:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://...\"},\n    \"title\": \"...\",\n    \"poster\": \"...\"\n};\n```\n\nHmm, then it would parse fine. The fallback branch exists for pages where options is not valid JSON after transformation.\n\nTo trigger the fallback in a test, I can make options_str something that js_to_json can't fix into valid JSON. For example:\n\n`var options = {video: {mp4: 'https://example.com/api'}, title: 'Test', poster: 'https://example.com/p.jpg'};`\n\njs_to_json would convert unquoted keys to quoted: `{\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}, \"title\": \"Test\", \"poster\": \"https://example.com/p.jpg\"}` \u2014 that would parse fine. Hmm.\n\nWhat if the values contain unquoted strings? Like `var options = {video: {mp4: https://example.com/api}};` \u2014 js_to_json wouldn't quote the value, so JSON parse fails. Then fallback:\n\n- `video_urls = self._parse_json(self._html_search_regex(r'\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,', trans_options_str, 'video urls'), 'video urls')` \u2014 hmm, this regex requires `\"video\"` with double quotes in trans_options_str. But if js_to_json failed to quote... hmm wait.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read. The regex is `r'\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,'` \u2014 it searches for `\"video\": {...},` in trans_options_str. So trans_options_str must contain `\"video\":` with double quotes. That means js_to_json did quote the key. But then the value inside must be unparseable.\n\nHmm, e.g.:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": https://example.com/api},\n    title: 'Test Title',\n    poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'\n};\n```\n\njs_to_json would quote `title` and `poster` keys, but