ct functions that record args and return canned formats, then assert the args.\n\nNow, about instantiating UDNEmbedIE: `UDNEmbedIE()` \u2014 InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None). Let me check if there's anything else in __init__. In youtube_dl/extractor/common.py:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, downloader=None):\n    self._ready = False\n    self._downloader = downloader\n    self._x_forwarded_for_ip = None\n```\n\nSomething like that. So `UDNEmbedIE()` works, with `self._downloader = None`.\n\nFor `_sort_formats`, if it needs `self._downloader.params`, I need to set `ie._downloader = FakeDownloader()` where FakeDownloader has `params = {}`. Or monkeypatch `ie._downloader`.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 maybe in this version of youtube_dl, `_sort_formats` doesn't use `self._downloader`. Let me check via get_info. I'll do that.\n\nAlso `_download_webpage` \u2014 I'll monkeypatch on the instance: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)`. Since it's an instance attribute assignment, monkeypatch.setattr works on instances too (it sets and restores).\n\nActually, monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) works for instance attributes as long as the attribute exists (it checks hasattr). Since `_download_webpage` is a method on the class, hasattr(ie, '_download_webpage') is True. monkeypatch will set instance attribute and delete it on teardown (it remembers it wasn't in the instance dict... actually monkeypatch.setattr records the old value and how to restore; for instance attributes where the attribute came from the class, it uses `delattr` on teardown? Let me think \u2014 pytest's monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value): it saves (target, name, oldval) and on undo does setattr(target, name, oldval). If oldval was from the class, setattr on the instance would shadow the class method with the bound method \u2014 that's fine functionally, though it leaves an instance attribute. Hmm, actually pytest monkeypatch handles this: it checks if name is in the object's __dict__... Let me recall the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(...)\n    # avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\n    if inspect.isclass(target):\n        oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\n    self._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nAnd undo:\n\n```python\ndef _undo(self):\n    for obj, name, value in reversed(self._setattr):\n        if value is not notset:\n            setattr(obj, name, value)\n        else:\n            delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nSo for an instance, oldval = getattr(ie, '_download_webpage') = bound method. On undo, setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', bound_method) \u2014 sets an instance attribute pointing to the bound method. That's harmless (no state pollution of the class). OK, fine.\n\nAlternatively, I can just assign directly without monkeypatch since the IE instance is created fresh in each test (no shared state). Creating a fresh instance per test and setting attributes on it is clean \u2014 no pollution. But the instructions say to use monkeypatch or pytest-mock if appropriate. Using a fresh instance per test is inherently clean. I'll use monkeypatch where it makes sense (e.g., patching methods on the fresh instance, or patching module-level functions).\n\nHmm, actually there's a subtlety: `js_to_json`, `determine_ext`, `int_or_none` are imported into the udn module namespace (`from youtube_dl.utils import ...`). The code shown imports them at module level. If I wanted to patch those, I'd patch `youtube_dl.extractor.udn.js_to_json`. But I don't need to patch them \u2014 they're pure functions that work fine.\n\nNow, the URL: `https://video.udn.com/embed/news/300040`. `_match_id` uses `_VALID_URL = r'https?://video\\.udn\\.com/(?:embed|play)/news/(?P<id>\\d+)'`. Let me verify the actual _VALID_URL via get_info on UDNEmbedIE._VALID_URL.\n\nLet me also double check `_download_webpage` signature to write the mock correctly: called as `self._download_webpage(url, video_id)` and `self._download_webpage(joined_url, video_id, note='retrieve url for %s video' % video_type)`. My mock: `def fake_download(url_or_request, video_id, *args, **kwargs)`.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `_real_extract` is what I should call directly, or whether I should go through `extract()`. `extract()` does more (sets ie_key, calls `_real_extract`, handles `_download_webpage`... no). Actually `extract(url)` calls `self._real_extract(url)` after checking `_match_valid_url` etc. Calling `_real_extract` directly is simpler and standard for such tests. But `extract` also wraps errors. I'll call `_real_extract` directly.\n\nHmm, but wait: does `extract` do `self.to_screen()` or use downloader? It might call `self._downloader.to_screen(...)`. Since _downloader is None, that would fail. So calling `_real_extract` directly is safer.\n\nNow let me write the fake downloader:\n\n```python\ndef _fake_download_webpage(monkeypatch, ie, mapping):\n    calls = []\n    def fake(url_or_request, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        calls.append((url_or_request, video_id))\n        # url_or_request may be a Request object? No \u2014 we pass str urls.\n        for pattern, response in mapping:\n            if pattern in str(url_or_request):\n                return response\n        raise AssertionError('Unexpected download: %s' % url_or_request)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)\n    return calls\n```\n\nWait, but the first call is `self._download_webpage(url, video_id)` with the page URL. The mapping should include the page URL \u2192 page HTML.\n\nNow the page HTML for test 2 (options path):\n\n```html\n<html><head><script>\nvar options = {'video': {'mp4_1080': '/api/1080', 'mp4_empty': '', 'mp4_other': '/api/other'}, 'title': 'Test Title', 'poster': 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'};\n</script></head><body></body></html>\n```\n\nThe regex `var\\s+options\\s*=\\s*([^;]+);` \u2014 `[^;]+` matches up to the first `;`. The options string contains no `;` inside (URLs don't have semicolons). Good. Note `[^;]+` is greedy but stops before `;`. So options_str = `{'video': {'mp4_1080': '/api/1080', 'mp4_empty': '', 'mp4_other': '/api/other'}, 'title': 'Test Title', 'poster': 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'}`.\n\njs_to_json on that: single quotes \u2192 double quotes. Result: `{\"video\": {\"mp4_1080\": \"/api/1080\", \"mp4_empty\": \"\", \"mp4_other\": \"/api/other\"}, \"title\": \"Test Title\", \"poster\": \"https://example.com/poster.jpg\"}`. Valid JSON. \n\nHmm, one concern: js_to_json's regex for keys \u2014 `{?\\s*(?:'[^']*'|\"[^\"]*\"|[^{}]+?)\\s*:` \u2014 for `'video'` it matches and fix_kv converts to `\"video\"`. For string values like `'/api/1080'` \u2014 does the key regex match them? The key regex requires a following `:`. `'/api/1080'` is followed by `,` not `:`. But there's also the first alternative: `\"(?:[^\"\\\\]*(?:\\\\\\\\|\\\\[\"\\\\]|[^\"]))*\"` \u2014 double-quoted strings. And single-quoted: `'(?:[^'\\\\]*(?:\\\\\\\\|\\\\['\\\\]|[^']))*'`. So single-quoted strings anywhere get converted to double-quoted. Good.\n\nWait, actually I need to double-check js_to_json in youtube_dl. Let me get it via get_info to be sure of its behavior with single quotes. I'm fairly confident it converts single-quoted strings to double-quoted and handles escapes. Yes.\n\nNow, `_parse_json(trans_options_str, 'options', fatal=False)` \u2192 dict. `if options:` \u2192 truthy. video_urls = options['video'] = {'mp4_1080': '/api/1080', 'mp4_empty': '', 'mp4_other': '/api/other'}. title = 'Test Title'. poster = 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'.\n\nvideo_urls.get('youtube') \u2192 None \u2192 falsy \u2192 continue.\n\nLoop:\n- ('mp4_1080', '/api/1080'): api_url truthy. urljoin('https://video.udn.com/embed/news/300040', '/api/1080') = 'https://video.u