 functions that record args and return canned formats, then assert the args.\n\nNow, about instantiating UDNEmbedIE: `UDNEmbedIE()` \u2014 InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None). Let me check if there's anything else in __init__. In youtube_dl/extractor/common.py:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, downloader=None):\n    self._ready = False\n    self._downloader = downloader\n    self._x_forwarded_for_ip = None\n```\n\nSomething like that. So `UDNEmbedIE()` works, with `self._downloader = None`.\n\nFor `_sort_formats`, if it needs `self._downloader.params`, I need to set `ie._downloader = FakeDownloader()` where FakeDownloader has `params = {}`. Or monkeypatch `ie._downloader`.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 maybe in this version of youtube_dl, `_sort_formats` doesn't use `self._downloader`. Let me check via get_info. I'll do that.\n\nAlso `_download_webpage` \u2014 I'll monkeypatch on the instance: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)`. Since it's an instance attribute assignment, monkeypatch.setattr works on instances too (it sets and restores).\n\nActually, monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) works for instance attributes as long as the attribute exists (it checks hasattr). Since `_download_webpage` is a method on the class, hasattr(ie, '_download_webpage') is True. monkeypatch will set instance attribute and delete it on teardown (it remembers it wasn't in the instance dict... actually monkeypatch.setattr records the old value and how to restore; for instance attributes where the attribute came from the class, it uses `delattr` on teardown? Let me think \u2014 pytest's monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value): it saves (target, name, oldval) and on undo does setattr(target, name, oldval). If oldval was from the class, setattr on the instance would shadow the class method with the bound method \u2014 that's fine functionally, though it leaves an instance attribute. Hmm, actually pytest monkeypatch handles this: it checks if name is in the object's __dict__... Let me recall the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef setattr(self, target, name, value=notset, raising=True):\n    ...\n    oldval = getattr(target, name, notset)\n    if raising and oldval is notset:\n        raise AttributeError(...)\n    # avoid class descriptors like staticmethod/classmethod\n    if inspect.isclass(target):\n        oldval = target.__dict__.get(name, notset)\n    self._setattr.append((target, name, oldval))\n    setattr(target, name, value)\n```\n\nAnd undo:\n\n```python\ndef _undo(self):\n    for obj, name, value in reversed(self._setattr):\n        if value is not notset:\n            setattr(obj, name, value)\n        else:\n            delattr(obj, name)\n```\n\nSo for an instance, oldval = getattr(ie, '_download_webpage') = bound method. On undo, setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', bound_method) \u2014 sets an instance attribute pointing to the bound method. That's harmless (no state pollution of the class). OK, fine.\n\nAlternatively, I can just assign directly without monkeypatch since the IE instance is created fresh in each test (no shared state). Creating a fresh instance per test and setting attributes on it is clean \u2014 no pollution. But the instructions say to use monkeypatch or pytest-mock if appropriate. Using a fresh instance per test is inherently clean. I'll use monkeypatch where it makes sense (e.g., patching methods on the fresh instance, or patching module-level functions).\n\nHmm, actually there's a subtlety: `js_to_json`, `determine_ext`, `int_or_none` are imported into the udn module namespace (`from youtube_dl.utils import ...`). The code shown imports them at module level. If I wanted to patch those, I'd patch `youtube_dl.extractor.udn.js_to_json`. But I don't need to patch them \u2014 they're pure functions that work fine.\n\nNow, the URL: `https://video.udn.com/embed/news/300040`. `_match_id` uses `_VALID_URL = r'https?://video\\.udn\\.com/(?:embed|play)/news/(?P<id>\\d+)'`. Let me verify the actual _VALID_