    _TEST = {\n        'url': 'https://video.udn.com/embed/news/300040',\n        'info_dict': {\n            'id': '300040',\n            'ext': 'mp4',\n            'title': '\u751f\u7269\u8001\u5e2b\u7537\u8b8a\u5973 \u5168\u53f0\u9996\u4f8b',\n            'thumbnail': r're:^https?://.*\\.jpg$',\n        },\n        'params': {\n            # m3u8 download\n            'skip_download': True,\n        },\n    }\n\n    def _real_extract(self, url):\n        video_id = self._match_id(url)\n\n        page = self._download_webpage(url, video_id)\n\n        options_str = self._html_search_regex(\n            r'var\\s+options\\s*=\\s*([^;]+);', page, 'options')\n        trans_options_str = js_to_json(options_str)\n        options = self._parse_json(trans_options_str, 'options', fatal=False) or {}\n        if options:\n            video_urls = options['video']\n            title = options['title']\n            poster = options.get('poster')\n        else:\n            video_urls = self._parse_json(self._html_search_regex(\n                r'\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,', trans_options_str, 'video urls'), 'video urls')\n            title = self._html_search_regex(\n                r\"title\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,\", options_str, 'title')\n            poster = self._html_search_regex(\n                r\"poster\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,\", options_str, 'poster', default=None)\n\n        if video_urls.get('youtube'):\n            return self.url_result(video_urls.get('youtube'), 'Youtube')\n\n        formats = []\n        for video_type, api_url in video_urls.items():\n            if not api_url:\n                continue\n\n            video_url = self._download_webpage(\n                compat_urlparse.urljoin(url, api_url), video_id,\n                note='retrieve url for %s video' % video_type)\n\n            ext = determine_ext(video_url)\n            if ext == 'm3u8':\n                formats.extend(self._extract_m3u8_formats(\n                    video_url, video_id, ext='mp4', m3u8_id='hls'))\n            elif ext == 'f4m':\n                formats.extend(self._extract_f4m_formats(\n                    video_url, video_id, f4m_id='hds'))\n            else:\n                mobj = re.search(r'_(?P<height>\\d+)p_(?P<tbr>\\d+)\\.mp4', video_url)\n                a_format = {\n                    'url': video_url,\n                    # video_type may be 'mp4', which confuses YoutubeDL\n                    'format_id': 'http-' + video_type,\n                }\n                if mobj:\n                    a_format.update({\n                        'height': int_or_none(mobj.group('height')),\n                        'tbr': int_or_none(mobj.group('tbr')),\n                    })\n                formats.append(a_format)\n\n        self._sort_formats(formats)\n\n        return {\n            'id': video_id,\n            'formats': formats,\n            'title': title,\n            'thumbnail': poster,\n        }\n```\n\nSo to test this, I need to:\n1. Create an instance of UDNEmbedIE (or use the real one from youtube_dl.extractor.udn)\n2. Mock `_download_webpage` to return controlled HTML\n3. Mock `_extract_m3u8_formats` and `_extract_f4m_formats` to return controlled formats\n4. Mock `_sort_formats` maybe, or let it run (it should work on real formats)\n5. Call `_real_extract(url)` and assert the result\n\nLet me think about the branches:\n\nBranch 1: `options` is truthy (parsed JSON succeeds) - lines 50-53\nBranch 2: `options` is falsy (parse fails) - lines 55-60\n\nBranch 3: `video_urls.get('youtube')` is truthy - lines 62-63 (early return)\nBranch 4: no youtube - continue to formats\n\nBranch 5: `api_url` is falsy - skip (lines 67-68)\nBranch 6: `api_url` is truthy - download\n\nBranch 7: ext == 'm3u8' - lines 75-77\nBranch 8: ext == 'f4m' - lines 78-80\nBranch 9: else - lines 82-93\nBranch 10: mobj is truthy (regex matches) - lines 88-91\nBranch 11: mobj is falsy - no update\n\nSo I need multiple tests:\n\nTest 1: options path with youtube URL \u2192 returns url_result\nTest 2: options path with mp4 URLs (with and without height/tbr pattern, and empty api_url) \u2192 returns formats\nTest 3: fallback path (options parse fails) with m3u8 and f4m URLs\nTest 4: maybe combine\n\nLet me think about how to instantiate the IE. The InfoExtractor's `__init__` takes a downloader. Let me check.\n\nActually, let me use get_info to check InfoExtractor's methods.\n\nLet me plan the test:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.udn import UDNEmbedIE\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor import YoutubeIE\n\n\nclass FakeIE(UDNEmbedIE):\n    ...\n```\n\nActually, simpler: create an instance and monkeypatch its methods.\n\n```python\ndef make_ie(monkeypatch, pages):\n    ie = UDNEmbedIE()\n    ...\n```\n\nInfoExtractor's `__init__` signature: `def __init__(self, downloader=None)`. So `UDNEmbedIE()` should work.\n\nBut `_download_webpage` does a lot of stuff (calls `_download_webpage_handle` which calls `self._opener`...). We need to monkeypatch `_download_webpage` on the instance.\n\nAlso `_html_search_regex` - that works on the page string, no network needed. `_parse_json` - pure. `js_to_json` - pure. `determine_ext` - pure.\n\n`_extract_m3u8_formats` and `_extract_f4m_formats` - these download the m3u8/f4m content, so we should monkeypatch them to return canned formats.\n\n`_sort_formats` - sorts formats by quality; it needs `_downloader` maybe? Let me check. `_sort_formats` calls `_sort_formats` from utils... Actually in InfoExtractor:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found')\n    formats.sort(key=_sort_formats_key)  # something like this\n```\n\nActually let me check the actual implementation. In youtube_dl (the old one), `_sort_formats` is:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found!')\n\n    def _formats_key(f):\n        # TODO remove the following workaround\n        ...\n```\n\nIt might reference `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')`. Let me check with get_info.\n\nAlso `url_result` - returns a dict with `_type: 'url'`, `url`, `ie_key`. It may call `self._downloader`? Let me check:\n\n```python\ndef url_result(self, url, ie=None, video_id=None, video_title=None):\n    \"\"\"Returns a url that points to a page that should be processed\"\"\"\n    # TODO: PEP 8 (two blank lines)\n    if video_id is None:\n        video_id = self._generic_id(url)\n    if video_title is not None:\n        video_id = '%s %s' % (video_id, video_title)\n    entry = {\n        '_type': 'url',\n        'url': url,\n        'id': video_id,\n    }\n    if ie is not None:\n        entry['ie_key'] = ie\n    return entry\n```\n\nThat's fine, no downloader needed.\n\n`_match_id` uses `self._match_valid_url(url)` which uses `_VALID_URL`. Fine.\n\nLet me check `_sort_formats` in youtube_dl. I'll use get_info.\n\nAlso, `_download_webpage` signature: `def _download_webpage(self, url_or_request, video_id, note=None, errnote=None, fatal=True, tries=1, timeout=5.0, data=None, headers={}, query={}, expected_status=None)`. We'll monkeypatch it.\n\nLet me now design the tests.\n\nTest A: youtube branch (options path)\n- page HTML contains `var options = {...};` where the JSON (after js_to_json) parses to a dict with 'video': {'youtube': 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxx'}, 'title': 'Test Title', 'poster': 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'.\n- Note: `js_to_json` converts single-quoted keys to double quotes etc. Actually `options_str` is like `{'video': {'youtube': '...'}, 'title': '...', 'poster': '...'}`. `js_to_json` converts it to valid JSON. Then `_parse_json` parses it.\n- Assert result == {'_type': 'url', 'url': 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxx', 'id': ..., 'ie_key': 'Youtube'}\n\nWait, `url_result` with video_id=None calls `self._generic_id(url)`. Let me check `_generic_id`:\n\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef _generic_id(url):\n    mobj = re.search(r'[^/?#]+(?=$|#[^#]*$)', url)  # something like this\n```\n\nActually:\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef _generic_id(url):\n    return re.search(r'[^/?#]+(?=$|#[^#]*$)', url).group()\n```\n\nHmm, not sure. Let me check with get_info. Actually in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef _generic_id(url):\n    mobj = re.search(r'[^/?#]+(?=$|#[^#]*$)', url)\n    if mobj:\n        return mobj.group()\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure. Let me just check via get_info.\n\nActually, for the youtube URL 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxx', `_generic_id` would return... the regex `[^/?#]+(?=$|#[^#]*$)` \u2014 hmm. Let me not guess; I'll check.\n\nActually, simpler: I can just assert `result['url'] == 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxx'` and `result['ie_key'] == 'Youtube'` and `result['_type'] == 'url'`. That avoids depending on `_generic_id` behavior.\n\nTest B: options path, mp4 formats\n- options: {'video': {'mp4_1080': 'api1', 'mp4_720': '', 'mp4_480': 'api2'}, 'title': 'T', 'poster': 'P'}\n- Wait, but the loop iterates over video_urls.items(). For 'mp4_1080' \u2192 api_url 'api1' (truthy), download \u2192 returns 'https://cdn.example.com/video_1080p_4000.mp4' \u2192 regex matches \u2192 height=1080, tbr=4000, format_id='http-mp4_1080'.\n- For 'mp4_720' \u2192 '' \u2192 skipped (continue).\n- For 'mp4_480' \u2192 'api2' \u2192 download \u2192 'https://cdn.example.com/video.mp4' (no pattern) \u2192 format without height/tbr.\n\nThen `_sort_formats` is called. Need to make sure it works. It might need `self._downloader` for params. Let me check the implementation.\n\nIn youtube_dl/extractor/common.py:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found!')\n\n    def _formats_key(f):\n        # TODO remove the following workaround\n        from ..utils import determine_ext\n        if f.get('ext') in (None, 'unknown_ext'):\n            f['ext'] = determine_ext(f['url'])\n\n        # TODO move all sanitization to _sort_formats\n        protocol = f.get('protocol')\n        if protocol is None:\n            protocol = determine_protocol(f)\n        f['protocol'] = protocol\n\n        preference = f.get('preference')\n        if preference is None:\n            preference = 0\n        if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm, it references `self._downloader.params`. If `self._downloader` is None, that would fail with AttributeError. So I need to set a fake downloader with a `params` dict.\n\nActually wait, in older youtube_dl versions, `_sort_formats` might be:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found!')\n\n    def _formats_key(f):\n        # TODO remove the following workaround\n        from ..utils import determine_ext\n        if f.get('ext') in (None, 'unknown_ext'):\n            f['ext'] = determine_ext(f['url'])\n\n        # TODO move all sanitization to _sort_formats\n        protocol = f.get('protocol')\n        if protocol is None:\n            protocol = determine_protocol(f)\n        f['protocol'] = protocol\n\n        preference = f.get('preference')\n        if preference is None:\n            preference = 0\n        if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n            ORDER = [preference, f.get('height') is None, f.get('height'), f.get('tbr') is None, f.get('tbr'), f.get('vcodec') == 'none', f.get('acodec') == 'none', f.get('abr') is None, f.get('abr'), f.get('ext') in ('f4f', 'f4m'), f.get('filesize') is None, f.get('filesize')]\n        else:\n            ORDER = [preference, f.get('vcodec') == 'none', f.get('height') is None, f.get('height'), f.get('tbr') is None, f.get('tbr'), f.get('acodec') == 'none', f.get('abr') is None, f.get('abr'), f.get('ext') in ('f4f', 'f4m'), f.get('filesize') is None, f.get('filesize')]\n        return tuple(ORDER)\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually I recall in youtube_dl the `_sort_formats` uses `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')`. Let me verify with get_info.\n\nActually, I recall that in newer versions of youtube-dl, `_sort_formats` is:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found!')\n\n    formats.sort(key=_sort_formats_key)\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me just check with the tool.\n\nAlso, the formats from `_extract_m3u8_formats` mock need to be dicts that `_sort_formats` can handle. If I mock `_extract_m3u8_formats` to return e.g. `[{'url': ..., 'format_id': 'hls-720', 'ext': 'mp4', 'protocol': 'm3u8', 'height': 720, 'tbr': 2000}]`, sorting should work.\n\nActually, `_sort_formats_key` might call `determine_protocol` which uses `f['url']`. Fine.\n\nLet me check the actual youtube_dl version's `_sort_formats`. I'll call get_info on '_sort_formats' and 'InfoExtractor._sort_formats'.\n\nAlso need to check `_parse_json` signature: `def _parse_json(self, json_string, video_id, transform_source=None, fatal=True, errnote=None, ignore_extra=False)`. Called with `self._parse_json(trans_options_str, 'options', fatal=False)`. Fine.\n\n`_html_search_regex(self, pattern, string, name, group=1, fatal=True, flags=0)`. Fine.\n\nNow, about `js_to_json`: it converts JS object literal to JSON. For `{'video': {'youtube': 'https://...'}, 'title': 'x', 'poster': 'y'}` it should produce valid JSON. Note js_to_json handles single quotes, unquoted keys, etc.\n\nFor the fallback branch (options parse fails), we need `trans_options_str` to NOT parse as JSON. E.g., options_str = `{'video': {...}, title: 'x', ...}` \u2014 hmm, js_to_json would convert unquoted keys to quoted. Hmm.\n\nWait, how does the fallback happen in reality? The real page has something like:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    video: {\"mp4\": \"...\", \"youtube\": \"...\"},\n    ...\n};\n```\n\nHmm, actually the fallback happens when `_parse_json` fails. With `fatal=False`, it returns None, then `or {}` makes it `{}`.\n\nTo make `_parse_json` fail, `trans_options_str` must be invalid JSON. But `js_to_json` is quite lenient... Let me think. `js_to_json` handles comments, single quotes, unquoted keys, trailing commas? Let me check.\n\nActually, in the real UDN page, the options might be like:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://...\"},\n    \"title\": \"...\",\n    \"poster\": \"...\"\n};\n```\n\nHmm, then it would parse fine. The fallback branch exists for pages where options is not valid JSON after transformation.\n\nTo trigger the fallback in a test, I can make options_str something that js_to_json can't fix into valid JSON. For example:\n\n`var options = {video: {mp4: 'https://example.com/api'}, title: 'Test', poster: 'https://example.com/p.jpg'};`\n\njs_to_json would convert unquoted keys to quoted: `{\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}, \"title\": \"Test\", \"poster\": \"https://example.com/p.jpg\"}` \u2014 that would parse fine. Hmm.\n\nWhat if the values contain unquoted strings? Like `var options = {video: {mp4: https://example.com/api}};` \u2014 js_to_json wouldn't quote the value, so JSON parse fails. Then fallback:\n\n- `video_urls = self._parse_json(self._html_search_regex(r'\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,', trans_options_str, 'video urls'), 'video urls')` \u2014 hmm, this regex requires `\"video\"` with double quotes in trans_options_str. But if js_to_json failed to quote... hmm wait.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read. The regex is `r'\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,'` \u2014 it searches for `\"video\": {...},` in trans_options_str. So trans_options_str must contain `\"video\":` with double quotes. That means js_to_json did quote the key. But then the value inside must be unparseable.\n\nHmm, e.g.:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": https://example.com/api},\n    title: 'Test Title',\n    poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'\n};\n```\n\njs_to_json would quote `title` and `poster` keys, but the value `https://example.com/api` remains unquoted \u2192 invalid JSON \u2192 `_parse_json` returns None (fatal=False) \u2192 fallback.\n\nThen:\n- regex `\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,` matches `{\"mp4\": https://example.com/api}` \u2014 wait, `({.+?})` is non-greedy, followed by `\\s*,`. The match would be `{\"mp4\": https://example.com/api}` followed by `,`. Hmm, but `.+?` non-greedy with `}` then `\\s*,`... The first `}` followed by `,` is at the end. So group(1) = `{\"mp4\": https://example.com/api}`. Then `_parse_json` on that... would fail! Because the value is unquoted.\n\nHmm wait, that's a problem. The fallback branch parses video_urls with `_parse_json` (fatal=True by default). So the video dict must be valid JSON. But the whole options must be invalid JSON.\n\nSo: the video dict itself is valid JSON (double-quoted), but something else in options makes the whole thing invalid. E.g.:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"},\n    title: 'Test Title',\n    poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'\n};\n```\n\njs_to_json quotes `title` and `poster` \u2192 `{\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}, \"title\": \"Test Title\", \"poster\": \"https://example.com/poster.jpg\"}` \u2014 that's valid JSON! Damn.\n\nHmm. So what makes js_to_json output invalid JSON? Let me think about js_to_json's behavior. It handles:\n- single-quoted strings \u2192 double-quoted\n- unquoted keys \u2192 quoted\n- comments\n- trailing commas? I think newer versions handle trailing commas.\n\nWhat about unquoted string values? js_to_json does NOT quote unquoted values (it can't know they're strings). So:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"},\n    title: Test Title,\n    poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'\n};\n```\n\njs_to_json: `title: Test Title` \u2192 `\"title\": Test Title` \u2014 invalid JSON (Test Title unquoted). So `_parse_json` fails \u2192 fallback.\n\nThen regex `\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,` on trans_options_str: trans_options_str = `{\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}, \"title\": Test Title, \"poster\": \"https://example.com/poster.jpg\"}`. The regex matches `\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"},` \u2192 group(1) = `{\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}` \u2192 parses fine \u2192 video_urls = {'mp4': 'https://example.com/api'}.\n\nThen title regex: `title\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,` on options_str (the ORIGINAL, not transformed). options_str = `{ \"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}, title: 'Test Title', poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg' }`. The regex `title\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,` matches `title: 'Test Title',` \u2192 group(1) = 'Test Title'. \n\nThen poster regex: `poster\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,` matches `poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg',`... wait, is there a trailing comma after poster? In my example, poster is the last entry, so no trailing comma. The regex requires `\\s*,` after the closing quote. So I need a comma after poster. Let me add another key after poster, or put a trailing comma:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"},\n    title: 'Test Title',\n    poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg',\n};\n```\n\nHmm, trailing comma \u2014 js_to_json might handle it or not. Actually if js_to_json handles trailing commas, then... the invalid part is still `Test Title` unquoted, so it still fails. Good.\n\nWait, but actually, does js_to_json handle trailing commas? In youtube_dl's utils:\n\n```python\ndef js_to_json(code):\n    def fix_kv(m):\n        v = m.group(0)\n        if v in ('true', 'false', 'null'):\n            return v\n        elif v.startswith('/*') or v == ',':\n            return \"\"\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, in newer versions there's handling for trailing commas. Let me check the version. Actually, let me check with get_info on js_to_json.\n\nAlternatively, to be safe, put poster not last:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"},\n    title: 'Test Title',\n    poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg',\n    other: 123\n};\n```\n\nHmm, but then js_to_json output: `{\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}, \"title\": Test Title, \"poster\": \"https://example.com/poster.jpg\", \"other\": 123}` \u2014 invalid due to `Test Title`. Good.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: does js_to_json quote unquoted values? Let me check the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef js_to_json(code):\n    def fix_kv(m):\n        v = m.group(0)\n        if v in ('true', 'false', 'null'):\n            return v\n        elif v.startswith('/*') or v == ',':\n            return \"\"\n\n        if v.startswith(\"'\"):\n            v = v[1:-1]\n            v = re.sub(r'\\\\([\"\\'/\\\\])', r'\\1', v)  # hmm not exactly\n            ...\n        return '\"%s\"' % v\n\n    return re.sub(r'''(?x)\n        \"(?:[^\"\\\\]*(?:\\\\\\\\|\\\\[\"\\\\]|[^\"]))*\"|   # double-quoted string\n        '(?:[^'\\\\]*(?:\\\\\\\\|\\\\['\\\\]|[^']))*'|   # single-quoted string\n        /\\*.*?\\*/|                             # multiline comment\n        ,(?\\s*[\\]}])|                          # trailing comma? (newer)\n        {?\\s*(?:'[^']*'|\"[^\"]*\"|[^{}]+?)\\s*:   # object key\n        ''', fix_kv, code)\n```\n\nSomething like that. The key part: `{?\\s*(?:'[^']*'|\"[^\"]*\"|[^{}]+?)\\s*:` matches keys. Values that are unquoted identifiers are NOT matched, so they remain as-is \u2192 invalid JSON. \n\nSo my plan for the fallback test works: have an unquoted value like `title: Test Title` \u2014 wait, but then the title regex `title\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,` requires single quotes around the title in options_str. So title must be single-quoted in options_str for the regex to match. But then js_to_json would convert it to double-quoted and it would be valid...\n\nHmm! Conflict: for the title regex to match, `title: 'Test Title',` must be in options_str. js_to_json converts `'Test Title'` to `\"Test Title\"`. So the transformed string has valid title. So the invalid part must be elsewhere.\n\nOK so let me make the invalid part be something else, e.g. an unquoted value for a different key:\n\n```js\nvar options = {\n    \"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"},\n    title: 'Test Title',\n    poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg',\n    autoplay: yes\n};\n```\n\njs_to_json: `autoplay: yes` \u2192 `\"autoplay\": yes` \u2192 invalid JSON \u2192 parse fails \u2192 fallback. \n\nThen:\n- video_urls regex on trans_options_str: `\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"},` \u2192 matches. Wait, need to double check the regex: `r'\"video\"\\s*:\\s*({.+?})\\s*,'`. trans_options_str contains `\"video\": {\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}, \"title\": \"Test Title\", ...`. The regex: `\"video\"` matches, `\\s*:\\s*` matches `: `, `({.+?})` non-greedy... `.+?` matches as little as possible, then `}` then `\\s*,`. The first `}` is at the end of `{\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}`. After it is `,`. So group(1) = `{\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}`. \n\nHmm wait, but `.+?` could also match across... no, non-greedy finds the earliest `}` followed by optional whitespace and `,`. The first `}` in the string is the one closing the mp4 dict. After it: `,`. So match is `{\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}`. \n\n- `_parse_json('{\"mp4\": \"https://example.com/api\"}', 'video urls')` \u2192 {'mp4': 'https://example.com/api'}. \n\n- title regex on options_str: `title\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,` \u2192 `title: 'Test Title',` \u2192 'Test Title'. \n\n- poster regex on options_str: `poster\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,` \u2192 `poster: 'https://example.com/poster.jpg',` \u2192 'https://example.com/poster.jpg'. \n\nThen continue to formats: video_urls = {'mp4': 'https://example.com/api'}. Download api \u2192 returns m3u8 URL \u2192 `_extract_m3u8_formats` (mocked) \u2192 formats. Then sort, return.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 I want to cover m3u8 and f4m branch