      "reasoning_content": "Very interesting! This is a DIFFERENT version of youtube_dl than the classic one. Key findings:\n\n1. `js_to_json`: The regex includes `(?:(?<![0-9])[eE]|[a-df-zA-DF-Z_])[.a-zA-Z_0-9]*` \u2014 this matches bare identifiers (like `yes`, `title`, `poster`, `autoplay`) and quotes them: `'\"%s\"' % v`. Wait \u2014 so bare words ARE quoted! Let me re-analyze.\n\nThe regex alternatives:\n- `\"(?:[^\"\\\\]*(?:\\\\\\\\|\\\\['\"nurtbfx/\\n]))*[^\"\\\\]*\"` \u2014 double-quoted strings\n- `'(?:[^'\\\\]*(?:\\\\\\\\|\\\\['\"nurtbfx/\\n]))*[^'\\\\]*'` \u2014 single-quoted strings\n- `{comment}` \u2014 comments\n- `,(?={skip}[\\]}])` \u2014 trailing commas \u2192 removed\n- `(?:(?<![0-9])[eE]|[a-df-zA-DF-Z_])[.a-zA-Z_0-9]*` \u2014 identifiers starting with letter (not e/E preceded by digit... hmm) or underscore \u2192 these get quoted with `\"%s\"`\n- `\\b(?:0[xX]...|0+[0-7]+)(?:{skip}:)?` \u2014 numbers\n- `[0-9]+(?={skip}:)` \u2014 numeric keys\n- `!+` \u2014 bangs\n\nAnd fix_kv: for identifier matches (not starting with quote), checks INTEGER_TABLE (hex/octal), else returns `'\"%s\"' % v`.\n\nWAIT \u2014 but then how do `true`, `false`, `null` survive? fix_kv checks `if v in ('true', 'false', 'null'): return v`. And identifiers like `yes` \u2192 `'\"yes\"'` \u2014 QUOTED! So `autoplay: yes` becomes `\"autoplay\": \"yes\"` \u2014 VALID JSON!\n\nHmm!! So my fallback plan breaks: js_to_json quotes bare identifiers. So how does `_parse_json` ever fail in the fallback branch?\n\nHmm. Let me think about what makes js_to_json output invalid JSON:\n- Numbers like `0123` (octal)? Handled.\n- Something like a value with special chars?\n- What about `-` in identifiers? The identifier regex `[a-df-zA-DF-Z_][.a-zA-Z_0-9]*` \u2014 doesn't include `-`. So `foo-bar` \u2192 `foo` matched, `-bar` not \u2192 `\"foo\"-bar` \u2192 invalid!\n- What about unbalanced quotes?\n- What about `+` concatenation? `'a' + 'b'` \u2192 `\"a\" + \"b\"` \u2192 invalid JSON! YES! String concatenation with `+` is common in JS and js_to_json does NOT handle it.\n\nSo for the fallback, I can use something like `title: 'Fall' + 'back Title'`? Hmm wait, but then the title regex `title\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,` on options_str \u2014 `'Fall' + 'back Title'` \u2014 the regex would match `title: 'Fall'` \u2192 group(1) = 'Fall' (since `.+?` non-greedy up to `'` followed by `\\s*,`... hmm, `'Fall' + 'back Title',` \u2014 the regex `title\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,` needs `'...'` immediately followed by `,`. First candidate: `'Fall'` followed by ` + ...` \u2014 not `,`. So regex engine continues: `.+?` expands... `'Fall' + 'back Title'` \u2014 hmm, the match must start at `title\\s*:\\s*'` \u2014 so after `title: ` we need `'(.+?)'\\s*,`. The first `'` is at 'Fall'. `(.+?)` = `Fall`, then `'`, then `\\s*,`? Next is ` + ` \u2014 no. Expand `(.+?)`: `Fall' + 'back Title` then `'` then `,` \u2713. So group(1) = `Fall' + 'back Title`. Ugly but works. Hmm, that's ugly for a title.\n\nAlternative invalid-JSON constructs:\n- `var options = {..., video: {...}, ...}` with a JS comment inside? Comments are handled (removed).\n- Trailing comma? Handled (removed).\n- Single quotes with escaped chars? Handled.\n- What about a value like `https://example.com/x` unquoted? The identifier regex: `(?:(?<![0-9])[eE]|[a-df-zA-DF-Z_])[.a-zA-Z_0-9]*` \u2014 `https` matches (`h` start, then `[.a-zA-Z_0-9]*` matches `ttps`) \u2192 `\"https\"`, then `://...` \u2014 `:` not matched, `/` not matched... \u2192 `\"https\"://example.com/x` \u2192 invalid JSON \u2713. But wait \u2014 `//example.com/x` looks like a `//` comment to the COMMENT_RE! `//[^\\n]*` \u2014 the comment alternative `//[^\\n]*` would match `//example.com/x` and REMOVE it! Hmm, order of alternatives in re.sub: the regex is a single alternation scanned left-to-right through the string. At the position of `//`, which alternative matches? The scan proceeds character by character; at each position, alternatives tried in order. Before reaching `//`, the identifier `https` is matched at its start. Then scanning resumes after `https`, at `://example.com/x`. At `:` \u2014 no alternative matches `:` alone... hmm, actually if no alternative matches at a position, re.sub just moves ahead one char. At `/` (first slash): try alternatives: double-quote? no. single? no. comment `{comment}` = `/\\*(?:(?!\\*/).)*?\\*/|//[^\\n]*` \u2014 `//...` matches! \u2192 removed. So `://example.com/x` \u2192 `:` then rest removed \u2192 `\"https\":` \u2192 then nothing. Result: `\"https\":` \u2192 invalid JSON (missing value) \u2713. Interesting but fragile.\n\nHmm, what about simply having a value that's a JS expression like `2+2`? `2+2` \u2192 `[0-9]+(?={skip}:)` \u2014 `2` followed by `+`... the lookahead `(?={skip}:)` requires `:` after optional space/comment \u2014 `+` doesn't match \u2192 alternative fails. Identifier? starts with digit \u2014 the identifier alt requires letter/underscore/e-E start... `(?:(?<![0-9])[eE]|[a-df-zA-DF-Z_])` \u2014 first char must be e/E (not preceded by digit) or a-d/f-z/A-Z/_ . `2` doesn't match. Number alt `\\b(?:0[xX]...|0+[0-7]+)...` \u2014 `2` \u2014 `0+[0-7]+` requires starting with 0. No. So `2` unmatched, `+` unmatched, `2` unmatched \u2192 stays `2+2` \u2192 invalid JSON \u2713\u2713. \n\nSo `autoplay: 2+2` hmm, or simpler: a math expression value. But honestly, the simplest robust approach: make the options string contain something js_to_json provably leaves invalid. Options:\n\n(a) String concatenation: `title: 'A' + 'B'` \u2192 `\"A\" + \"B\"` \u2192 invalid \u2713. But messes with title regex as analyzed (title becomes `A' + 'B`... wait let me redo: options_str contains `title: 'A' + 'B',`. Regex `title\\s*:\\s*'(.+?)'\\s*,`: after `title: `, match `'`, then `(.+?)` minimal, then `'\\s*,`. Try `(.+?)`='A', then `'`, then `\\s*,`? After `'A'` comes ` + 'B',` \u2014 `\\s*` matches ` `, then need `,` but have `+`. Fail. Expand: `(.+?)` = `A' + 'B`, then `'`, then `,` \u