ntain a duplicate key or something json hates... no, `2+2` is fine. Or use `+` in a \"userAgent\" style value... Let me go with a JS expression.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually there's an even more natural one: an UNQUOTED URL as a value, e.g. `link: https://example.com/page,`. As analyzed, `https` \u2192 `\"https\"`, then `://example.com/page` \u2014 the `//...` part gets eaten as a comment \u2192 `\"link\": :` hmm wait: `https://example.com/page` \u2192 identifier `https` \u2192 `\"https\"`; then `:` stays; then `//example.com/page` \u2192 comment \u2192 removed. Result: `\"link\": :` \u2192 invalid \u2713. But two colons... json.loads fails \u2713. Still, `2+2` is more predictable. Go with `2+2`.\n\nHmm, hold on, wait. Let me re-examine: is it possible that js_to_json turns `2+2` into something valid? No \u2014 as traced, `2+2` is untouched. json.loads('\"volume\": 2+2}') \u2014 wait the full string: `{\"video\": {...}, \"title\": \"T\", \"poster\": \"P\", \"volume\": 2+2}` \u2192 json.loads fails at `2+2` \u2713 (ValueError: Extra data or invalid). Actually `2+2` \u2014 json parses `2`, then expects `,` or `}` but finds `+` \u2192 ValueError \u2713.\n\n2. `_sort_formats` \u2014 GREAT news, this version is totally different:\n\n```python\ndef _sort_formats(self, formats, field_preference=None):\n    if not formats:\n        raise ExtractorError('No video formats found')\n    for f in formats:\n        if 'tbr' not in f and f.get('abr') is not None and (f.get('vbr') is not None):\n            f['tbr'] = f['abr'] + f['vbr']\n\n    def _formats_key(f):\n        from ..utils import determine_ext\n        if not f.get('ext') and 'url' in f:\n            f['ext'] = determine_ext(f['url'])\n        if isinstance(field_preference, (list, tuple)):\n            ...\n        preference = f.get('preference')\n        if preference is None:\n            preference = 0\n            if f.get('ext') in ['f4f', 'f4m']:\n                preference -= 0.5\n        protocol = f.get('protocol') or determine_protocol(f)\n        proto_preference = 0 if protocol in ['http', 'https'] else -0.5 if protocol == 'rtsp' else -0.1\n        if f.get('vcodec') == 'none':\n            preference -= 50\n            ...\n        else:\n            if f.get('acodec') == 'none':\n                preference -= 40\n            ...\n            ext_preference = ORDER.index(f['ext']) ...\n            audio_ext_preference = 0\n        return (preference, language_preference, quality, tbr, filesize, vbr, height, width, proto_preference, ext_preference, abr, audio_ext_preference, fps, filesize_approx, source_preference, format_id)\n    formats.sort(key=_formats_key)\n```\n\nKEY observations:\n- `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')` is accessed ONLY inside `if f.get('vcodec') == 'none':` branch. Our formats have no 'vcodec' \u2192 else branch \u2192 `self._downloader` NOT touched! Wait, let me recheck: \n\n```python\n        if f.get('vcodec') == 'none':\n            preference -= 50\n            if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n                ORDER = [...]\n            else:\n                ORDER = [...]\n            ext_preference = 0\n            try:\n                audio_ext_preference = ORDER.index(f['ext'])\n            ...\n        else:\n            if f.get('acodec') == 'none':\n                preference -= 40\n            if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n```\n\nHmm wait, the else branch ALSO has `if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):` \u2014 looking at the returned source:\n\n```python\n            if f.get('vcodec') == 'none':\n                preference -= 50\n                if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n                    ORDER = ['aac', 'mp3', 'm4a', 'webm', 'ogg', 'opus']\n                else:\n                    ORDER = ['webm', 'opus', 'ogg', 'mp3', 'aac', 'm4a']\n                ext_preference = 0\n                try:\n                    audio_ext_preference = ORDER.index(f['ext'])\n                except ValueError:\n                    audio_ext_preference = -1\n            else:\n                if f.get('acodec') == 'none':\n                    preference -= 40\n                if self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats'):\n                    ORDER = ['flv', 'mp4', 'webm']\n                else:\n                    ORDER = ['webm', 'flv', 'mp4']\n                try:\n                    ext_preference = ORDER.index(f['ext'])\n                except ValueError:\n                    ext_preference = -1\n                audio_ext_preference = 0\n```\n\nYes \u2014 BOTH branches access `self._downloader.params.get('prefer_free_formats')`. So `self._downloader` MUST be non-None with a `params` dict. \u2192 FakeDownloader with `params = {}` is REQUIRED. \n\n- Ordering: key tuple = (preference, lang, quality, tbr, filesize, vbr, height, width, proto_pref, ext_pref, abr, audio_ext_pref, fps, filesize_approx, source_pref, format_id), sorted ASCENDING, missing \u2192 -1. So HIGHER tbr/height \u2192 LARGER key \u2192 LATER in list. Best last \u2713 (classic youtube-dl semantics).\n\nSo for test 2 (mp4 formats):\n- high: ext 'mp4' (set by workaround from url), preference 0 (ext 'mp4' not in ['f4f','f4m']), protocol: not provided \u2192 determine_protocol \u2192 'https' \u2192 proto_preference 0. vcodec != 'none' \u2192 else: acodec != 'none' \u2192 no -40. prefer_free_formats falsy \u2192 ORDER = ['webm', 'flv', 'mp4'] \u2192 ext_preference = index('mp4') = 2. audio_ext_preference = 0.\n  key = (0, -1, -1, 4000, -1, -1, 1080, -1, 0, 2, -1, 0, -1, -1, -1, 'http-mp4_1080')\n- plain: ext 'mp4', preference 0, tbr -1, height -1, ext_pref 2.\n  key = (0, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1, 0, 2, -1, 0, -1, -1, -1, 'http-mp4_other')\n\nCompare: tbr 4000 vs -1 \u2192 high > plain \u2192 plain FIRST, high LAST. So formats[-1] == 'http-mp4_1080' \u2713 (best last). \n\nFor test 3 (hls/hds):\n- hls-720: ext 'mp4', pr